\section{Line types}{
A constant \code{linetype} (tmap: \code{lty}) is carried over to the symbol's
stroke — the body of a line, the border of a polygon, the ring around
a point marker. \code{"solid"}, \code{"dashed"}, \code{"dotted"} and \code{"dotdash"}
(1-4 in numeric form) map to the matching QGIS pen styles.
\code{"longdash"}, \code{"twodash"} and the hex on/off patterns (e.g. \code{"1343"})
have no QGIS preset: on a line layer they become the equivalent custom
dash pattern (scaled by the line width, like in R), while polygon
borders and marker rings, which only support the preset pen styles,
get the nearest preset instead. \code{"blank"} (0) turns a polygon border
or marker ring off; a blank line layer would draw nothing and is an
error. A linetype that varies by feature (a mapped \code{linetype}/\code{lty})
cannot be represented by these renderers: the symbols are drawn with
solid lines, with a warning.
}

\section{Point symbols}{
A point layer's \code{shape} becomes the QGIS marker of the same outline:
pch 0/15/22 a \code{square}, 1/16/19/20/21 a \code{circle}, 5/18/23 a \code{diamond},
2/17/24 an \code{equilateral_triangle} (6/25 the same, rotated 180 degrees),
3 a \code{cross} and 4 a \code{cross2}; ggplot2's shape names (\code{"circle filled"}, ...) are those same symbols. The composite symbols
(pch 7-14), R's thin asterisk (8), a single-character shape (which
draws a text glyph) and tmap's grob shapes have no QGIS counterpart and
are errors rather than a silently different marker.

How R colors the shape decides where the layer's colors go: pch 0-6 are
stroke-only, so the marker gets a transparent interior and its
\code{colour}; pch 15-20 are filled with a single color (ggplot2's \code{colour},
tmap's \code{fill}, see \emph{tmap symbol constants}) and get no distinct border;
pch 21-25 fill with \code{fill} and stroke with \code{colour}. Mapping a color to
the slot the shape does not draw is an error.

The marker's size is the symbol's extent, which each package computes
its own way: ggplot2 spans \code{size} millimeters plus two thirds of
\code{stroke}, tmap a multiple of the text line height (see \emph{tmap symbol
constants}). QGIS is then given the width the shape's ink actually
spans — three quarters of that extent for a circle — so a default
ggplot2 point (\code{size = 1.5}, \code{stroke = 0.5}, pch 19) is a 1.38 mm
circle. Its ring is \code{stroke} wide in R's line-width units, i.e.
0.25 mm by default; \code{linewidth}, which the point geoms do not have,
plays no part.

A \code{size}, \code{shape} or \code{stroke} that varies by feature (a mapped
aesthetic) is drawn with the first feature's value, with a warning: the
QGIS renderers vary the color and nothing else.
}

\section{Opacity}{
A constant \code{alpha} (tmap: \code{fill_alpha} and \code{col_alpha}) becomes the
alpha component of the colors it applies to, rather than QGIS's
symbol-wide opacity — which could not render a translucent fill under
an opaque border. Which colors it applies to is what the plotting
package draws with it: ggplot2 gives a polygon's interior its \code{alpha}
and leaves the border opaque, applies it to a line's own color, and to
both colors of a point marker; tmap's two alphas belong to \code{fill} and
\code{col} respectively. Like the other constants, an \code{alpha} that varies by
feature is dropped down to the first feature's value, with a warning.

An alpha of 0 means "not drawn", like an NA color, and a layer with
nothing left to draw is an error. The classes of a graduated or
categorized renderer carry the alpha too, as does the data-defined
color expression of \code{gradient_style = "continuous"}, but the
renderer's \verb{[source]} color ramp stays opaque: it is the palette rather
than the rendering opacity, so re-classifying the layer in QGIS drops
the transparency. An alpha a color carries itself
(\code{colour = "#FF000080"}) is not carried over — only its RGB part is.
}

\section{Text and labels}{
A \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_sf_text]{ggplot2::geom_sf_text()}} or \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_sf_label]{ggplot2::geom_sf_label()}} layer becomes
a separate labels-only QGIS layer: its features are not drawn (a
null-symbol renderer) and the \code{label} aesthetic — a bare column name,
like \code{fill}/\code{colour} — becomes the labeled field, rendered by QGIS's
own labeling engine. \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_text]{ggplot2::geom_text()}} and \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_label]{ggplot2::geom_label()}}
work the same way for plain data frames, under the \emph{Data frame layers}
rules (the plot must use \code{\link[ggplot2:coord_sf]{ggplot2::coord_sf()}}, \code{x}/\code{y} must be bare
untransformed columns, identity stat and position — so nudging is not
supported).

The carried-over styles are the text size (in the layer's \code{size.unit}),
the font family and the text color and, for the label geoms, the
background fill color (\code{geom_label()}'s \code{fill}; drawn as a plain
rectangle, \code{fill = NA} disables it). R's default sans-serif family
(\code{""} or \code{"sans"}) is a device alias rather than a font name, so it
leaves QGIS its own default font. Everything else (fontface, rounded
corners, hjust/vjust, alpha, ...) keeps the QGIS labeling defaults, and
the label placement is QGIS's: point labels are drawn over the point,
line labels along and \emph{on} the line (rather than QGIS's default of
above it, since ggplot2 centers its text on the geometry), polygon
labels around the centroid.

A layer whose features are drawn under its own labels — that is
\code{geom_spatraster_contour_text()}, see \emph{SpatRaster layers} — masks
them, so the text is not overdrawn by the line it labels. The
labels-only layers of the text/label geoms have nothing to mask.
}

\section{Data frame layers}{
A \code{geom_point()}, \code{geom_path()}, \code{geom_line()} or \code{geom_polygon()} layer
drawn from a plain data frame is converted to an sf layer: one point per
row, or one linestring/polygon per group (ggplot2's grouping — an
explicit \code{group} aesthetic or the interaction of the discrete
aesthetics; \code{geom_line()} orders each line by \code{x} like ggplot2 does,
and polygon rings are closed). The plot must use \code{\link[ggplot2:coord_sf]{ggplot2::coord_sf()}},
and the \code{x}/\code{y} values are taken to be coordinates in the panel CRS:
\code{coord_sf()}'s \code{crs} argument if given, otherwise the CRS of the first
sf layer (\code{coord_sf(default_crs = )} is not supported). Like
\code{fill}/\code{colour}, the \code{x}/\code{y} aesthetics must be bare column names, and
the layer must use the identity stat and position.

A point layer keeps every column of the data frame as attributes; a
line/polygon layer keeps the columns that are constant within every
group, one feature per group (a mapped \code{fill}/\code{colour} column must be
constant within each group). \code{geom_polygon()}'s default \code{colour} is
\code{NA}, so such a layer is written without a border. A
\code{geom_point()} layer's \code{size}, \code{shape}, \code{stroke} and \code{alpha} are
carried over like a \code{geom_sf()} point layer's (see \emph{Point symbols}
and \emph{Opacity}).

The project opens zoomed to the plot's displayed range (the panel range,
including the default expansion and any \code{\link[ggplot2:coord_sf]{ggplot2::coord_sf()}} \code{xlim}/
\code{ylim}), reprojected to the project CRS, rather than the whole world.
}

\section{SpatRaster layers}{
A \code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatraster]{tidyterra::geom_spatraster()}} layer becomes a QGIS raster layer:
the SpatRaster is written as a GeoTIFF (\verb{<layer name>.tif}, named
after the band by default) next to the GeoPackages, and rendered by a
single-band pseudocolor renderer reproducing the plot's continuous
\code{fill} scale as an interpolated color ramp. Unlike for vector layers
the ramp is exact \emph{and} the legend shows it as a continuous ramp, so
the \code{gradient_style} option does not apply to raster layers. Cells
with missing values are transparent (the GeoTIFF's nodata value).

A \code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatraster_rgb]{tidyterra::geom_spatraster_rgb()}} layer becomes a QGIS raster
layer with a multiband (true color) renderer. The written GeoTIFF
holds the three bands in red-green-blue order, with the layer's
\code{r}/\code{g}/\code{b} band selection, \code{zlim}/\code{stretch} rescaling and the
constant \code{alpha} (the renderer's opacity) carried over. A
\code{max_col_value} other than 255 becomes a linear contrast stretch
from \verb{0..max_col_value}, matching how tidyterra scales the channel
values.

What is written is the data the plot draws: a raster larger than
the geom's \code{maxcell} argument (500,000 cells by default)
has already been downsampled by tidyterra when the layer was created
and the original is not recoverable from the plot object — so the
GeoTIFF stays small, but is not the full-resolution source. For
\code{geom_spatraster()}, only a single-band SpatRaster with a continuous
(not binned) \code{fill} scale and the default \code{fill} mapping (the band
value) is supported for now; tidyterra's color tables
(\code{scale_fill_coltab()}) are not.

The contour geoms are the exception among the SpatRaster geoms: they
draw vector shapes, so they become ordinary GeoPackage-backed layers.
\code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatraster_contour]{tidyterra::geom_spatraster_contour()}} becomes a LineString layer with
one feature per contour line (per level and per piece), and
\code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatraster_contour_filled]{tidyterra::geom_spatraster_contour_filled()}} a Polygon layer with one
MULTIPOLYGON feature per band, the holes punched by the bands above it
kept as holes. Both keep the band name as a \code{lyr} attribute and the
contour value as a \code{level} one — a number for the lines, the band's
label (e.g. \code{"(70, 80]"}) for the filled bands. The shapes are the ones
the layer's stat computed, so the geom's \code{breaks}/\code{bins}/\code{binwidth} are
reproduced as they are — and, since the stat reprojects the raster to
the plot's CRS before contouring, the layer's CRS is the plot's, not
the raster's.

\code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatraster_contour_text]{tidyterra::geom_spatraster_contour_text()}} draws the same isolines with
their value written along them, so it becomes that same LineString
layer with QGIS labeling enabled (see \emph{Text and labels}). The text is
written as a \code{label} attribute — the \code{label} aesthetic (the contour
value by default) run through the geom's \code{label_format}, so a custom
format or a vector of labels is reproduced as it is; \code{label_format = NULL}, which tells the geom to place no labels, leaves the layer
unlabeled. The labels mask the lines they are written into, which is
how the gap ggplot2 breaks in each line under its label is reproduced.

On a \code{geom_spatraster_contour()} layer, \code{colour} may be mapped to
\code{after_stat(level)}, which becomes a renderer on the \code{level} attribute
under the usual \code{gradient_style} rules. A
\code{geom_spatraster_contour_filled()} layer always varies its \code{fill} (the
stat maps it to \code{after_stat(level)}), which becomes a categorized
renderer, one category per level of the fill scale. \code{level} is the only
computed value written, so any other \code{colour}/\code{fill} expression is an
error, as is a mapping the geom does not draw (\code{fill} on the lines and
on the text, \code{colour} on the bands). On the text geom, \code{colour} colors
the labels as well as the lines, and a QGIS labeling carries a single
text color: the lines follow the scale and every label is drawn in the
first feature's color, with a warning (silently when the layer has no
labels to begin with). The constant \code{colour}, \code{linewidth}, \code{linetype}
and \code{alpha} are carried over like any other line or polygon layer's. A
multi-band SpatRaster is not supported.

The layers are named \code{"<band>_contour"}, \code{"<band>_contour_text"} and
\code{"<band>_contour_filled"} by default, so overlaying the contours on the
raster itself gives distinguishable layers.

\code{geom_spatraster()} appends an invisible helper layer (a single empty
point carrying the raster's CRS to \code{coord_sf()}). Such a layer draws
nothing, so it is not written to the project: in general, an sf layer
whose geometries are all empty is skipped and does not count for
\code{layer_names}.
}

\section{SpatVector layers}{
\code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatvector]{tidyterra::geom_spatvector()}}, \code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatvector_text]{tidyterra::geom_spatvector_text()}} and
\code{\link[tidyterra:geom_spatvector_label]{tidyterra::geom_spatvector_label()}} are wrappers of
\code{\link[ggplot2:geom_sf]{ggplot2::geom_sf()}}, \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_sf_text]{ggplot2::geom_sf_text()}} and
\code{\link[ggplot2:geom_sf_label]{ggplot2::geom_sf_label()}}, and tidyterra registers a \code{fortify()} method
that turns a \code{SpatVector} into an sf object when the plot or the layer is
created. Such a layer is therefore an ordinary sf layer here, converted
by all the rules above, and so is a plain \code{geom_sf()} layer given a
\code{SpatVector} as its data. \code{terra::vect()} typically produces a geometry
column mixing the single and \code{MULTI} variants of one type (e.g.
\code{POLYGON} and \code{MULTIPOLYGON}), which is cast to the \code{MULTI} variant
since a GeoPackage table holds a single geometry type.

One consequence of the wrapping shows up in the derived layer names:
ggplot2 records the \emph{wrapped} \code{geom_sf()} call as the layer's
constructor, so the name comes from the variable the \code{SpatVector} is
bound to rather than from the call. When there is no such variable (e.g.
\code{geom_spatvector(data = terra::vect(path))}), the geom fallback names
the layer \code{geom_sf}, not \code{geom_spatvector} — the wrapper's own name is
not recorded anywhere in the layer. Pass \code{layer_names} for full control.
}

\section{tmap plots}{
A tmap (>= 4.4) object is converted the same way:
\code{tm_polygons()}/\code{tm_fill()}/\code{tm_borders()}, \code{tm_lines()}, and
\code{tm_symbols()}/\code{tm_dots()}/\code{tm_bubbles()}/\code{tm_squares()} (on point data)
are supported, including \code{\link[tmap:qtm]{tmap::qtm()}} maps, as is \code{\link[tmap:tm_raster]{tmap::tm_raster()}}
on a raster shape (see \emph{tmap raster layers}). The color scales are
reproduced from tmap's own trained scales:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_intervals]{tmap::tm_scale_intervals()}} (any classification style) becomes a
graduated renderer with tmap's exact break boundaries and colors
(zero-width bins from tied breaks are collapsed; the continuous-style
legend variants, \code{label.style}, are not supported),
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_categorical]{tmap::tm_scale_categorical()}} and \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_ordinal]{tmap::tm_scale_ordinal()}} become a
categorized renderer keyed by the raw data values (missing values
become the "all other values" category); tmap's formatted legend
labels are not carried over,
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_continuous]{tmap::tm_scale_continuous()}} (including the transformed variants,
approximated with piecewise-linear color stops) becomes a graduated
renderer with 25 equal-interval classes, or an exact continuous
gradient with \code{gradient_style = "continuous"}.
}

A layer maps either \code{fill} or \code{col} to a data column (not both). The
constants tmap computed for the layer — \code{lwd}, \code{lty} (see \emph{Line
types}), \code{size} and \code{shape} (see \emph{Point symbols} and \emph{tmap symbol
constants}), \code{fill_alpha} and \code{col_alpha} (see \emph{Opacity}) — are carried
over; a constant that varies between features is dropped down to its
first value, with a warning.
Layers sharing one \code{\link[tmap:tm_shape]{tmap::tm_shape()}} share one GeoPackage: the data is
written once and every layer of the shape references the same table.

QGIS's graduated renderers have no missing-value class, so for an
intervals/continuous scale the features whose value is missing go to a
separate layer named \code{"<layer> (missing value)"}, drawn in tmap's
\code{value.na} color directly below its parent layer (the same GeoPackage
table, filtered with the QGIS provider filter \verb{"column" IS NULL}). Set
\code{create_na_layer = FALSE} to leave the missing features undrawn
instead. Categorical scales render missing values within their own
layer (the "all other values" category), so they never get the extra
layer. \code{\link[tmap:tm_basemap]{tmap::tm_basemap()}} layers become XYZ tile layers
(overriding the \code{basemap} argument): a URL template is used as is, a
provider name is resolved via \code{\link[maptiles:get_providers]{maptiles::get_providers()}}, and with
several basemaps only the first one is checked (visible) in the layer
tree. Facets, \code{tm_text()} and the other scale types are errors.

Map decorations are dropped: \code{\link[tmap:tm_graticules]{tmap::tm_graticules()}}/\code{\link[tmap:tm_grid]{tmap::tm_grid()}},
and the components tmap draws around the map
(\code{\link[tmap:tm_compass]{tmap::tm_compass()}}, \code{\link[tmap:tm_scalebar]{tmap::tm_scalebar()}}, \code{\link[tmap:tm_title]{tmap::tm_title()}},
\code{\link[tmap:tm_credits]{tmap::tm_credits()}}, ...). They carry no data of their own, and QGIS
offers its own equivalents. \code{\link[tmap:tm_tiles]{tmap::tm_tiles()}} is \emph{not} dropped but an
error: it draws content that would silently go missing.

A caveat on the bin edges: tmap's interval bins are left-closed
(\verb{[a, b)}) while every QGIS classed renderer is right-closed
(\verb{(a, b]}), so a feature or cell whose value falls exactly on an
interior break renders one class lower in QGIS than in tmap.

The project CRS defaults to the tmap display CRS (\code{use_plot_crs = TRUE}
for tmap objects): [tmap::tm_crs()] or the main shape's CRS — or
EPSG:3857 when the map has basemaps, which is how tmap itself resolves
it — so the project opens looking like the tmap plot, zoomed to the
main shape's bounding box. \code{use_plot_crs = FALSE} forces EPSG:3857.

The conversion relies on tmap internals that are not part of its public
API, so a tmap version older than 4.4 is rejected.
}

\section{tmap symbol constants}{
What \emph{Point symbols} leaves to each package is the symbol's extent:
tmap's \code{size} is a multiple of the text line height, which is 5.08 mm
on an R device with the default 12 pt font, so a default
\code{tm_symbols()} marker is a 3.81 mm circle (three quarters of it) and a
\code{tm_dots()} one 1.143 mm. A device opened with a different \code{pointsize}
would draw tmap's symbols at a different physical size; the conversion
assumes the default. [tmap::tm_layout()]'s \code{scale} is already part of
the size tmap computes, so it carries over.

tmap moves \code{col} into \code{fill} before drawing a pch 15-20 symbol (R fills
those with \code{col}), so the single color of such a marker is tmap's
\code{fill} — which is also why mapping \code{col} on one is an error.
}

\section{tmap raster layers}{
\code{\link[tmap:tm_raster]{tmap::tm_raster()}} on a raster shape (a stars object, a SpatRaster or
a RasterLayer passed to \code{\link[tmap:tm_shape]{tmap::tm_shape()}}) becomes a QGIS raster
layer, written as a single-band GeoTIFF next to the project. The
renderer comes from the same trained scale as for vector layers:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_intervals]{tmap::tm_scale_intervals()}} (the default for a numeric variable)
becomes a \code{singlebandpseudocolor} renderer with a DISCRETE color-ramp
shader holding tmap's exact breaks, colors and bin labels,
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_categorical]{tmap::tm_scale_categorical()}} and \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_ordinal]{tmap::tm_scale_ordinal()}} become
a \code{paletted} renderer with one entry per value. A factor variable is
written as its integer codes and labeled with the level names;
values that no cell has are dropped,
\item \code{\link[tmap:tm_scale_continuous]{tmap::tm_scale_continuous()}} becomes a \code{singlebandpseudocolor}
renderer with an INTERPOLATED shader, which reproduces the gradient
exactly and shows a continuous legend ramp — so \code{gradient_style} does
not apply to raster layers.
}

What is written is the grid tmap \emph{draws}: tmap downsamples a raster
beyond \code{tmap_options(raster.max_cells =)} and reprojects it to the
display CRS before the scales are trained, and the original is not
recoverable from the plot object.

Missing cells become the GeoTIFF's nodata value and are painted in
tmap's \code{value.na} color through the renderer's \code{nodataColor} — exactly,
so raster layers never get the separate \code{"(missing value)"} layer that
vector layers do and \code{create_na_layer} does not apply to them. A
constant \code{col_alpha} becomes the layer opacity; a per-cell one is an
error. Unlike vector layers, raster layers of one \code{\link[tmap:tm_shape]{tmap::tm_shape()}} do
\emph{not} share a file: each writes its own single-band GeoTIFF, since two
variables can differ in value type and nodata value.

[tmap::tm_rgb()]/[tmap::tm_rgba()], [tmap::tm_scale_discrete()], and
curvilinear, rotated or irregularly spaced grids are errors.

